        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "<p>None</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-30T18:18:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57985,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hibernomas are most often located in the thigh, trunk, chest, upper extremity, and head and neck. Myxoid and spindle cell subtypes often involve the posterior neck and shoulder {11395560}. Fewer than 10% of cases occur in intra-abdominal, retroperitoneal, or thoracic locations. Very rare primary hibernomas of bone chiefly involve the axial skeleton {28827513}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57986,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hibernomas typically present in young adults (mean age: 38&nbsp;years; range: 2&ndash;75&nbsp;years), as small, slow-growing, painless, mobile, subcutaneous masses {11395560}. Fewer than 20% are intramuscular. They are slightly more common in males. On MRI, they are isointense or hypointense to fat on T1, isointense to hyperintense to fat on T2, and isointense to hyperintense to muscle on short-tau inversion recovery (STIR). They often contain low-signal internal strands and may be more heterogeneous, depending on their composition. Enhancement is common and more avid in lesions with a greater percentage of brown fat. Hibernomas are highly metabolically active and are frequently detected incidentally during FDG&nbsp;PET performed for the staging of various malignancies. There are two reports of hibernomas arising in patients with multiple endocrine neoplasia type&nbsp;1 {25309600; 28478946}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57987,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hibernomas account for &lt;&nbsp;2% of benign and 1% of all adipocytic tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57988,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is an association with multiple endocrine neoplasia type&nbsp;1 {25309600}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57989,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytogenetically, almost all hibernomas have breakpoints in chromosome arm&nbsp;11q, with a distinctive clustering to 11q13 {10393837; 12800150; 8056446; 21078971}. FISH, SNP array, and multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification analyses have revealed complex genomic rearrangements, with translocations and interstitial deletions that affect both homologues of chromosome&nbsp;11 {10393837; 12800150; 21078971}. The ultimate consequence of these rearrangements is a set of deletions that cluster to a 3&nbsp;Mb region in 11q13, with a preferential localization to regions covering the tumour suppressor genes <em>MEN1</em> and <em>AIP</em>. Somatic single-nucleotide variants in <em>MEN1</em> and <em>AIP</em> have not been detected in hibernoma {28722204}. Hibernomas strongly express the brown fat marker gene <em>UCP1</em>; rearrangement, amplification, or increased expression of <em>HMGA2</em>, <em>CDK4</em>, or <em>MDM2</em> is not detected {19508721; 29629919}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57990,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, hibernomas are typically well circumscribed, vaguely lobular, and brown to yellow in appearance {11395560}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57991,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hibernomas have a rich capillary network and are composed of eosinophilic and pale, polygonal, multivacuolated, granular, brown fat cells, with a variable component of univacuolated white fat. The nuclei of the brown fat cells are small, round, and centrally located, with small nucleoli. Classic hibernomas contain &gt;&nbsp;70% brown fat, with lipoma-like subtypes containing more white fat. Stromal myxoid change and an increased number of stromal spindled cells resembling those of spindle cell lipoma may be seen {11395560}. Nuclear atypia and mitotic activity are absent {29629919}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57992,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytology reveals uniform small multivacuolated brown fat cells with finely granular cytoplasm, bland round nuclei, and branching capillaries {11391608}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57993,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57994,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> tumour composed of multivacuolated brown fat cells with small, normochromatic, centrally located nuclei.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57995,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57996,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hibernomas are entirely benign and typically do not recur after local excision {11395560}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:08:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
